---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PaginationController from '../../components/PaginationController.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Database ACID Properties — Deep Dive · Hand Notes"
  description="Complete deep dive into ACID: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability. Covers MySQL internals, isolation levels, MVCC, anomalies, and interview questions."
>

<!-- ═══ COVER ═══ -->
<div class="cover">
  <div class="cover-subject">Database Fundamentals · Deep Dive Series</div>
  <h1>ACID Properties and Transaction Guarantees</h1>
  <div class="cover-sub">From theory to MySQL internals — everything you need to know</div>
  <div class="cover-tags">
    <span class="tag tag-g">Atomicity</span>
    <span class="tag tag-b">Consistency</span>
    <span class="tag tag-o">Isolation</span>
    <span class="tag tag-p">Durability</span>
    <span class="tag tag-g">MVCC</span>
    <span class="tag tag-b">Undo/Redo Logs</span>
  </div>
  <div class="cover-doodle">◉</div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 1: What is a Transaction ═══ -->
<div class="page green-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 01</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">★</span>
    <span class="section-title">1. What is a Transaction?</span>
    <span class="section-num">01</span>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text">
    <p>A <span class="hl">transaction</span> is a single logical unit of work containing one or more database operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, SELECT).</p>
    <p>Think of it as a <span class="wu">promise</span>: either <strong>ALL</strong> operations inside it succeed together, or <strong>NONE</strong> of them take effect.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">★ Real-World Analogy</div>
    <p>Transferring $5000 from your Savings Account to your Friend's Account involves TWO operations: <strong>Debit yours</strong> → <strong>Credit theirs</strong>. If the system crashes between steps, your money is gone and friend got nothing. A <strong>transaction</strong> says: "Either BOTH happen, or NEITHER happens."</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">SQL Transaction Syntax</h2>
  <pre class="code-block green-left"><span class="kw">START TRANSACTION</span>;

  <span class="kw">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class="kw">SET</span> balance = balance - <span class="st">5000</span> <span class="kw">WHERE</span> user_id = <span class="st">1</span>;  <span class="cm">-- Debit</span>
  <span class="kw">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class="kw">SET</span> balance = balance + <span class="st">5000</span> <span class="kw">WHERE</span> user_id = <span class="st">2</span>;  <span class="cm">-- Credit</span>

<span class="kw">COMMIT</span>;     <span class="cm">-- Make it permanent</span>
<span class="cm">-- or</span>
<span class="kw">ROLLBACK</span>;  <span class="cm">-- Undo everything if something went wrong</span></pre>

  <h2 class="sub">Why Do We Need ACID?</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Databases face three major threats that ACID protects against:</p></div>
  <div class="cheat-grid">
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--red);border-top-color:var(--red)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">System Crashes</div>
      <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--ink2)">Power failures, OS crashes, hardware faults</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--orange);border-top-color:var(--orange)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">Concurrent Users</div>
      <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--ink2)">Race conditions, dirty reads, lost updates</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cheat-card" style="color:var(--purple);border-top-color:var(--purple)">
      <div class="cheat-card-title">Partial Failures</div>
      <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--ink2)">Network errors, app bugs, disk errors</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight green-ins" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="insight-label">★ ACID = Four Guarantees</div>
    <p><span class="hl-green">A</span>tomicity → All or Nothing · <span class="hl-blue">C</span>onsistency → Rules are never broken · <span class="hl-orange">I</span>solation → Transactions don't interfere · <span class="hl-purple">D</span>urability → Committed data survives crashes</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 2: Atomicity ═══ -->
<div class="page orange-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 02</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">◎</span>
    <span class="section-title">2. Atomicity — All or Nothing</span>
    <span class="section-num">02</span>
  </div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">★ Definition</div>
    <p>Atomicity guarantees that a transaction is treated as a <strong>single indivisible unit</strong> — either ALL operations succeed, or ALL are rolled back. There is no partial success.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text"><p>The word "Atom" comes from Greek <em>atomos</em> meaning "indivisible." A transaction is the atom of the database world.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">The Bank Transfer Example</h2>
  <div class="code-compare">
    <div class="code-compare-panel">
      <div class="code-compare-label r">❌ Without Atomicity</div>
      <pre class="code-block orange-left"><span class="cm">Transfer $500 from Alice to Bob</span>

Step 1: Debit Alice  → Alice: $1000 → $500  ✅

       💥 CRASH HERE (power failure)

Step 2: Credit Bob   → NEVER EXECUTED       ❌

Result: Alice lost $500. Bob got nothing.
        $500 vanished from the system. 🔥</pre>
    </div>
    <div class="code-compare-sep">— vs —</div>
    <div class="code-compare-panel">
      <div class="code-compare-label g">✓ With Atomicity</div>
      <pre class="code-block green-left"><span class="cm">Transfer $500 from Alice to Bob</span>

Step 1: Debit Alice  → Staged (not committed)

       💥 CRASH HERE (power failure)

On Recovery: Database detects incomplete txn
             → Automatically ROLLS BACK Step 1

Result: Alice still has $1000.
        Data is safe. Consistent. ✅</pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">How MySQL Implements Atomicity — Undo Log</h2>
  <div class="diag-wrap">
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-box fb-gray">BEGIN TXN</div>
      <div class="flow-arr">→</div>
      <div class="flow-box fb-blue">Undo Log<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">stores old values first</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arr">→</div>
      <div class="flow-box fb-green">Data<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">gets changed</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <ul class="note-list">
    <li class="sq">Before any change, MySQL writes the <span class="hl-blue">ORIGINAL value</span> to the Undo Log</li>
    <li class="ck">If <code>COMMIT</code> → Undo Log entries can be discarded → changes permanent</li>
    <li class="ck">If <code>ROLLBACK</code> or CRASH → MySQL replays Undo Log in reverse → data restored</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="insight orange-ins">
    <div class="insight-label">★ Key Points</div>
    <p>Atomicity is about <strong>failure handling</strong>, not concurrency. It's enforced even on system crashes, not just explicit ROLLBACK. MySQL's <code>AUTOCOMMIT = 1</code> (default) means every single statement is its own transaction.</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 3: Consistency ═══ -->
<div class="page blue-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 03</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">✓</span>
    <span class="section-title">3. Consistency — Rules Never Broken</span>
    <span class="section-num">03</span>
  </div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">★ Definition</div>
    <p>Consistency ensures that a transaction brings the database from one <strong>valid state</strong> to another valid state, never leaving it in an invalid intermediate state. All defined rules, constraints, and triggers must be satisfied.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">What "Valid State" Means</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>A database defines rules about what data is acceptable:</p></div>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Constraint</th><th>Rule it enforces</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>CHECK</code></td><td>account balance &gt;= 0</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FOREIGN KEY</code></td><td>order must reference a valid customer</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>UNIQUE</code></td><td>email must be unique</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NOT NULL</code></td><td>name cannot be null</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Custom triggers</td><td>age must be &gt;= 18 for certain tables</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="body-text"><p>Consistency says: <span class="wu">these rules must ALWAYS hold true</span>. No transaction can leave the database in a state that violates these rules.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">Example — CHECK Constraint</h2>
  <pre class="code-block"><span class="kw">CREATE TABLE</span> accounts (
  id      <span class="cl">INT</span> <span class="kw">PRIMARY KEY</span>,
  balance <span class="cl">DECIMAL</span>(10,2),
  <span class="kw">CONSTRAINT</span> balance_positive <span class="kw">CHECK</span> (balance &gt;= 0)  <span class="cm">-- Rule: no negative</span>
);

<span class="cm">-- Alice has $100, trying to send $200</span>
<span class="kw">START TRANSACTION</span>;
  <span class="kw">UPDATE</span> accounts <span class="kw">SET</span> balance = balance - <span class="st">200</span> <span class="kw">WHERE</span> id = <span class="st">1</span>;
  <span class="cm">-- balance = -100 VIOLATES CHECK → MySQL raises error</span>
  <span class="cm">-- Transaction is ROLLED BACK automatically</span>
<span class="kw">COMMIT</span>;

<span class="cm">-- Result: Balance stays at $100. Rule preserved. ✅</span></pre>

  <h2 class="sub">Consistency Is a Shared Responsibility</h2>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title b">Database Engine</div>
      <ul class="note-list" style="font-size:14px;margin:0">
        <li class="ck">Enforces constraints (CHECK, FK, UNIQUE)</li>
        <li class="ck">Enforces data types</li>
        <li class="ck">Enforces triggers</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title g">Application Developer (YOU)</div>
      <ul class="note-list" style="font-size:14px;margin:0">
        <li class="ck">Write correct business logic</li>
        <li class="ck">Define correct constraints</li>
        <li class="ck">Use transactions properly</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="insight-label">! Important Note</div>
    <p>Consistency is the <strong>"C" that YOU define</strong>. The database upholds it only based on the constraints you've declared. If you don't define a constraint, the database doesn't know the rule.</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 4: Isolation Anomalies ═══ -->
<div class="page purple-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 04</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">✗</span>
    <span class="section-title">4. Isolation — Concurrency Anomalies</span>
    <span class="section-num">04</span>
  </div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">★ Definition</div>
    <p>Isolation ensures that the intermediate state of a transaction is <strong>invisible to other concurrent transactions</strong>. Each transaction executes as if it is the only one running.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text"><p>Isolation is the <span class="hl">most complex</span> of the four ACID properties. It deals with <span class="wu">concurrency</span>. When multiple transactions run simultaneously, several anomalies can occur:</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">Anomaly 1: Dirty Read</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Reading data modified by another transaction that has <span class="hl-orange">NOT yet committed</span>.</p></div>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th style="width:60px">Time</th><th>Transaction A (Writer)</th><th>Transaction B (Reader)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>T1</strong></td><td><code>BEGIN</code></td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T2</strong></td><td><code>UPDATE</code> balance = 500 <em>(not yet committed)</em></td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T3</strong></td><td></td><td><code>BEGIN</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T4</strong></td><td></td><td><code>SELECT</code> balance → reads <span class="hl-orange">500</span> ❌ <strong>DIRTY!</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T5</strong></td><td><code>ROLLBACK</code> ← crashed</td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T6</strong></td><td>balance reverts to <span class="hl-green">1000</span></td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T7</strong></td><td></td><td>Uses $500 → <strong>WRONG DECISION ✗</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Transaction B read data that <strong>NEVER officially existed</strong>.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">Anomaly 2: Non-Repeatable Read</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Within the same transaction, reading the <span class="hl-orange">same row twice</span> gives <span class="hl-orange">different results</span>.</p></div>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th style="width:60px">Time</th><th>Transaction A (Reader)</th><th>Transaction B (Writer)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>T1</strong></td><td><code>BEGIN</code></td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T2</strong></td><td><code>SELECT</code> balance → <span class="hl-green">1000</span> ✓</td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T3</strong></td><td></td><td><code>BEGIN</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T4</strong></td><td></td><td><code>UPDATE</code> balance = 500</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T5</strong></td><td></td><td><code>COMMIT</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T6</strong></td><td><code>SELECT</code> balance → <span class="hl-orange">500</span> ❌ <em>(different — same txn!)</em></td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T7</strong></td><td><code>COMMIT</code></td><td></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Transaction A saw <strong>TWO different values</strong> in the <strong>SAME transaction</strong>.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">Anomaly 3: Phantom Read</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>The <span class="hl-orange">same query returns different rows</span> because another transaction inserted/deleted rows.</p></div>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th style="width:60px">Time</th><th>Transaction A (Reader)</th><th>Transaction B (Writer)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>T1</strong></td><td><code>BEGIN</code></td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T2</strong></td><td><code>SELECT COUNT(*) WHERE age &gt; 18</code> → <span class="hl-green">5 rows</span> ✓</td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T3</strong></td><td></td><td><code>BEGIN</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T4</strong></td><td></td><td><code>INSERT</code> new row, age = 25</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T5</strong></td><td></td><td><code>COMMIT</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T6</strong></td><td><code>SELECT COUNT(*) WHERE age &gt; 18</code> → <span class="hl-orange">6 rows</span> ❌ <em>(phantom appeared!)</em></td><td></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Same query, <strong>different row count</strong> — a "phantom" row materialized mid-transaction.</p></div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 5: Lost Update + Isolation Levels ═══ -->
<div class="page orange-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 05</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">≡</span>
    <span class="section-title">5. Lost Update &amp; Isolation Levels</span>
    <span class="section-num">05</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">Anomaly 4: Lost Update</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Two transactions read the same value, both modify it, and one update <span class="hl-orange">overwrites</span> the other.</p></div>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th style="width:60px">Time</th><th>Transaction A</th><th>Transaction B</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>T1</strong></td><td><code>READ</code> stock = <span class="hl-green">10</span></td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T2</strong></td><td></td><td><code>READ</code> stock = <span class="hl-green">10</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T3</strong></td><td>stock = 10 − 1 = <strong>9</strong></td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T4</strong></td><td></td><td>stock = 10 − 1 = <strong>9</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T5</strong></td><td><code>WRITE</code> stock = 9</td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>T6</strong></td><td></td><td><code>WRITE</code> stock = 9</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="insight orange-ins" style="margin-top:12px">
    <div class="insight-label">✗ Outcome</div>
    <p><strong>Expected:</strong> stock = 8 (two items sold) · <strong>Actual:</strong> stock = <span class="hl-orange">9</span> — one update was silently lost.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">Isolation Levels</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>SQL defines four isolation levels — each trades off <span class="hl-green">performance</span> against <span class="hl-blue">protection from anomalies</span>:</p></div>

  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Isolation Level</th><th>Dirty Read</th><th>Non-Repeatable</th><th>Phantom</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>READ UNCOMMITTED</code></td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>READ COMMITTED</code></td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Possible</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>REPEATABLE READ</code> ★</td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--orange)">Possible*</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SERIALIZABLE</code></td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td><td style="color:var(--green)">Prevented</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink3)">* InnoDB prevents phantoms at REPEATABLE READ using <span class="hl-blue">Gap Locks</span> (better than SQL standard)</p></div>

  <div class="diag-wrap" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-box fb-green">READ<br />UNCOMMITTED<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">fastest / weakest</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arr">→</div>
      <div class="flow-box fb-blue">READ<br />COMMITTED</div>
      <div class="flow-arr">→</div>
      <div class="flow-box fb-orange">REPEATABLE<br />READ ★<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">MySQL default</span></div>
      <div class="flow-arr">→</div>
      <div class="flow-box fb-purple">SERIALIZABLE<br /><span style="font-size:11px;opacity:0.7">slowest / strongest</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight green-ins" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="insight-label">★ MySQL Default</div>
    <p><strong>REPEATABLE READ</strong> is MySQL's default isolation level. InnoDB additionally prevents phantom reads using <strong>Gap Locks</strong>, which is stricter than the SQL standard requires.</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 6: MVCC ═══ -->
<div class="page green-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 06</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">↺</span>
    <span class="section-title">6. How MySQL Implements Isolation — MVCC</span>
    <span class="section-num">06</span>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text">
    <p>MySQL InnoDB uses <span class="hl-green">Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC)</span> — instead of locking rows for reads, it maintains <strong>multiple versions of rows</strong>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">Hidden Row Metadata</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Every row in InnoDB carries two <span class="hl-green">hidden columns</span> alongside your real data — they're what make MVCC possible:</p></div>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>name</th>
        <th>balance</th>
        <th>DB_TRX_ID</th>
        <th>DB_ROLL_PTR</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>"Alice"</code></td>
        <td><code>1000</code></td>
        <td><code>TXN_50</code></td>
        <td>→ Undo log pointer</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title b">DB_TRX_ID</div>
      <div class="body-text" style="font-size:14px;margin:0">The transaction ID that last modified this row.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title b">DB_ROLL_PTR</div>
      <div class="body-text" style="font-size:14px;margin:0">Pointer to the previous version of this row in the Undo Log.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">Read View — Snapshot Isolation</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>When a transaction starts reading, it builds a <span class="hl-blue">Read View</span> — a snapshot that locks in which row versions it is allowed to see.</p></div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">★ The Read View Rule</div>
    <p>"I will only see rows where <code>DB_TRX_ID</code> is <strong>older than my own transaction ID</strong>." Newer versions are filtered out, so the reader gets a stable, consistent snapshot.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="phase-flow" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="phase-box ph-blue">
      <div class="ph-num">Step 1 · Reader</div>
      <div class="ph-title">TXN_100 starts</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">Read View: only see rows where <code>DB_TRX_ID &lt; 100</code></div>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="phase-box ph-orange">
      <div class="ph-num">Step 2 · Writer</div>
      <div class="ph-title">TXN_101 updates the row</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">New version <code>DB_TRX_ID = 101</code> · old version preserved in Undo Log</div>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="phase-box ph-green">
      <div class="ph-num">Step 3 · Result</div>
      <div class="ph-title">Reader still sees the old version</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">No locks · no waiting · writers don't block readers</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="sticky" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="sticky-label">★ MVCC Key Insight</div>
    <p>MVCC allows <strong>readers and writers to work concurrently</strong> without locking each other. Readers see a consistent snapshot, writers create new versions. This is why InnoDB can handle high-concurrency workloads efficiently.</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 7: Durability ═══ -->
<div class="page purple-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 07</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">★</span>
    <span class="section-title">7. Durability — Committed Forever</span>
    <span class="section-num">07</span>
  </div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">★ Definition</div>
    <p>Durability guarantees that once a transaction is committed, it will remain committed <strong>permanently</strong> — even in the case of system failures like crashes, power outages, or errors. "Committed means committed." Forever.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text"><p>Same scenario, two different worlds — see what happens after the crash:</p></div>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th style="width:60px">Step</th><th>✗ Without Durability</th><th>✓ With Durability</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>1</strong></td>
        <td>You transfer $10,000</td>
        <td>You transfer $10,000</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>2</strong></td>
        <td>Database says <code>"COMMIT successful ✓"</code></td>
        <td>Database says <code>"COMMIT successful ✓"</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>3</strong></td>
        <td>⚡ Power goes out</td>
        <td>⚡ Power goes out</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>4</strong></td>
        <td><span class="hl-orange"><strong>Transfer never happened 😱</strong></span></td>
        <td><span class="hl-green"><strong>Transfer is still there ✓</strong></span></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink3)">Steps 1 – 3 are identical. Durability is what makes step 4 turn out differently.</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">The Redo Log &amp; Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>MySQL guarantees durability through <span class="hl-purple">Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)</span> — every change is recorded in the Redo Log <em>before</em> it ever touches a data file.</p></div>

  <div class="sticky">
    <div class="sticky-label">★ The WAL Golden Rule</div>
    <p><strong>"WRITE to the log BEFORE writing to the actual data file."</strong> The Redo Log is the source of truth — if a crash happens, MySQL replays it on restart and reconstructs every committed change.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="sub">The Commit-Critical Path</h3>
  <div class="phase-flow">
    <div class="phase-box ph-orange">
      <div class="ph-num">Step 1</div>
      <div class="ph-title">App calls commit()</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">MySQL receives the COMMIT — change still only in memory buffers</div>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="phase-box ph-purple">
      <div class="ph-num">Step 2</div>
      <div class="ph-title">Redo Log written</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">Record appended to log buffer, then to the OS file</div>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="phase-box ph-green">
      <div class="ph-num">Step 3 · Durable! ✓</div>
      <div class="ph-title">fsync() to disk platter</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">Only now does the COMMIT return success to your application</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight" style="margin-top:12px">
    <div class="insight-label">★ Why data pages don't block COMMIT</div>
    <p>The actual <code>.ibd</code> data pages get rewritten <strong>lazily, in the background</strong> — they don't block the COMMIT. The Redo Log already has everything needed to reconstruct unwritten pages on restart, so MySQL can return success the moment the log is safely on disk.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">The innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit Setting</h2>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Value</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Risk / Performance</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Write + flush once per SECOND</td><td style="color:var(--red)">Up to 1s data loss · fastest</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code> ★</td><td>Write + flush on EVERY commit</td><td style="color:var(--green)">Zero data loss · slowest</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>Write to OS cache per commit, flush per second</td><td style="color:var(--orange)">Loss only if OS crashes · middle</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight green-ins" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="insight-label">★ Production Recommendation</div>
    <p>Keep <code>innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit = 1</code> for true ACID compliance. Only change it if you understand the durability trade-offs and your workload can tolerate potential data loss.</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 8: MySQL ACID Stack ═══ -->
<div class="page blue-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 08</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">≡</span>
    <span class="section-title">8. InnoDB's Complete ACID Stack</span>
    <span class="section-num">08</span>
  </div>

  <div class="body-text"><p>Here is the complete picture of how InnoDB implements all four ACID properties:</p></div>

  <div class="phase-flow">
    <div class="phase-box ph-gray">
      <div class="ph-num">Layer 1</div>
      <div class="ph-title">APPLICATION</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">START TRANSACTION ... COMMIT</div>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="phase-box ph-blue">
      <div class="ph-num">Layer 2</div>
      <div class="ph-title">TRANSACTION MANAGER</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">Assigns TXN_ID · Manages BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK</div>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="phase-box ph-orange">
      <div class="ph-num">Layer 3</div>
      <div class="ph-title">UNDO LOG + REDO LOG + LOCK MANAGER</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">Atomicity &amp; MVCC · Durability · Isolation</div>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="phase-box ph-purple">
      <div class="ph-num">Layer 4</div>
      <div class="ph-title">BUFFER POOL</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">Data Pages cached in RAM</div>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-arrow">↓</div>
    <div class="phase-box ph-green">
      <div class="ph-num">Layer 5</div>
      <div class="ph-title">DISK</div>
      <div class="ph-sub">.ibd data files · ib_logfile* (redo logs)</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">ACID Property → InnoDB Mechanism</h2>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Property</th><th>Protects Against</th><th>InnoDB Mechanism</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="hl-green">Atomicity</span></td><td>Partial failures</td><td><strong>Undo Log</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="hl-blue">Consistency</span></td><td>Rule violations</td><td><strong>Constraints + App Logic</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="hl-orange">Isolation</span></td><td>Concurrency bugs</td><td><strong>MVCC + Locks + Read Views</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="hl-purple">Durability</span></td><td>System crashes</td><td><strong>Redo Log (WAL) + fsync</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="sticky" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="sticky-label">⚡ Crash Recovery Flow</div>
    <p>On restart → MySQL reads Redo Log → Re-applies committed transactions that didn't make it to data files → Reads Undo Log → Rolls back incomplete transactions → Database is consistent again ✓</p>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══ PAGE 9: ACID Violations + ACID vs BASE ═══ -->
<div class="page red-spine">
  <div class="page-num">pg 09</div>
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-icon">✗</span>
    <span class="section-title">9. ACID Violations &amp; ACID vs BASE</span>
    <span class="section-num">09</span>
  </div>

  <h2 class="sub">What Goes Wrong Without ACID</h2>
  <table class="note-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>System</th><th>Missing</th><th>Consequence</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Banking</td><td style="color:var(--red)">No Atomicity</td><td>Money created or destroyed during partial transfers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>E-Commerce</td><td style="color:var(--red)">No Consistency</td><td>Negative inventory: selling items you don't have</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ticket Booking</td><td style="color:var(--red)">No Isolation</td><td>Double-booking: two users buy the last seat</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payment System</td><td style="color:var(--red)">No Durability</td><td>Payment confirmed but disappears after restart</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 class="sub">ACID vs BASE</h2>
  <div class="body-text"><p>Modern distributed/NoSQL databases sometimes relax ACID for higher availability. This is called <span class="hl-orange">BASE</span>:</p></div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title g">ACID</div>
      <ul class="note-list" style="font-size:14px;margin:0">
        <li class="sq"><strong>A</strong>tomicity</li>
        <li class="sq"><strong>C</strong>onsistency</li>
        <li class="sq"><strong>I</strong>solation</li>
        <li class="sq"><strong>D</strong>urability</li>
        <li class="ck" style="margin-top:8px">Strong consistency</li>
        <li class="ck">Pessimistic approach</li>
        <li class="ck">MySQL, PostgreSQL</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="col-card">
      <div class="col-card-title o">BASE</div>
      <ul class="note-list" style="font-size:14px;margin:0">
        <li class="sq"><strong>B</strong>asically <strong>A</strong>vailable</li>
        <li class="sq"><strong>S</strong>oft state</li>
        <li class="sq"><strong>E</strong>ventually consistent</li>
        <li class="sq" style="opacity:0">—</li>
        <li class="ck" style="margin-top:8px">Eventual consistency</li>
        <li class="ck">Optimistic approach</li>
        <li class="ck">Cassandra, DynamoDB, MongoDB*</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="body-text"><p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink3)">* MongoDB supports ACID transactions since version 4.0</p></div>

  <h2 class="sub">The CAP Theorem Connection</h2>
  <pre class="code-block"><span class="cm">       Consistency</span>
            /\
           /  \
          /    \
         /  ?   \
        /________\
<span class="cm">Availability    Partition Tolerance</span>

CAP Theorem: In a distributed system, you can only guarantee 2 of 3.

<span class="hl-green">ACID databases</span>: Prefer C (Consistency) over A (Availability)
<span class="hl-orange">BASE databases</span>: Prefer A (Availability) over C (Consistency)</pre>
</div>


