  <h2 class="sub">Common Interview Questions</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li><strong>Q: What does ACID stand for?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability. Each addresses a different aspect of transaction reliability.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: Difference between Atomicity and Durability?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Atomicity = all-or-nothing (during failures). Durability = persists after commit (after crashes).</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: What is a Dirty Read?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Reading uncommitted data. If that transaction rolls back, you read data that never existed.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: MySQL's default isolation level?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">REPEATABLE READ. InnoDB also prevents phantoms via Gap Locks.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: How does MySQL implement Atomicity?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Undo Log. Original values written first. On rollback/crash, replays to restore.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: What is MVCC?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Multi-Version Concurrency Control. Multiple row versions, readers see snapshot, no lock contention.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Q: Phantom vs Non-Repeatable Read?</strong><br /><span style="font-size:14px;color:var(--ink2)">Non-Repeatable: same row, different value. Phantom: same query, different row count.</span></li>
  </ul>

